    "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Position Vector of a Particle Moving With Constant Acceleration",
    "problem": "A particle starts moving from the point with position vector $-2j\\,\\text{ft}$ with an acceleration of $4i\\,{\\text{ft/min}}^{2}$ and an initial velocity of $(3i+j)\\,\\text{ft/min}.$ Find the position vector of the particle after $2$ minutes.",
    "worked_solution": "If a particle moves with constant acceleration $a,$ the position vector of the particle at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nr={r}_{0}+ut+\\frac{1}{2}a{t}^{2},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${r}_{0}$ is the initial position of the particle and $u$ is its initial velocity.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{0}=-2j,\\,u=3i+j,\\,a=4i.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position vector of the particle after $t=2\\,\\text{min}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =(-2j)+(3i+j)(2)+\\frac{1}{2}(4i)(2)^{2} \\\\  & =-2j+(6i+2j)+2(4i) \\\\  & =6i+8i \\\\  & =14i\\,\\text{ft}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
